// Arbitrary-precision signed integers.
//
// A BigInt is a sign flag plus a little-endian vector of limbs. Limb zero is
// the least significant. Every limb holds LIMB_BITS bits, so a limb value is
// always in the half-open range [0, LIMB_BASE). Products of two limbs plus a
// carry stay inside the machine word, which is what lets the inner loops be
// written as single long expressions instead of a ladder of temporaries.

// --- Representation constants --------------------------------------------

// Bits per limb. Chosen so that LIMB_BASE * LIMB_BASE plus two carries still
// fits comfortably in a 64 bit accumulator.

// 2^30. Written in decimal because the lexer only accepts decimal literals.

// 2^30 - 1, the mask that reduces an accumulator down to one limb.

// Half of LIMB_BASE, used by the rounding helpers.

// Decimal conversion works nine digits at a time: 10^9 < 2^30 is false, so we
// use eight digits per chunk to keep the chunk strictly below LIMB_BASE.

// 10^8, the base of one decimal chunk.

// Below this limb count schoolbook multiplication beats Karatsuba because the
// recursion bookkeeping dominates the limb products.

